# ByteBikri — copy to `.env` and fill in what you need. `.env` is gitignored.
#
# Nothing here is required for a local checkout: the demo runs on the
# development defaults and the local database. Listed in the order the config
# check reads them.

# ── Signing secrets (required in production; `npm run secrets` generates them) ──
# SESSION_SECRET=
# ACCESS_TOKEN_SECRET=
# AD_POSTBACK_SECRET=

# ── The public origin, and the operator account ──────────────────────────────
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://example.com
# OPERATOR_EMAIL=
# OPERATOR_LEGAL_NAME=
# OPERATOR_ADDRESS=

# ── Database and email ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:pass@host:5432/bytebikri
# EMAIL_DRIVER=resend
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# EMAIL_FROM=

# ── Video storage (VIDEO_STORAGE.md) ────────────────────────────────────────
# Where a store's files are kept, and WHICH KIND goes where. Unset means local
# disk, which is the default: every byte stays here and nothing else changes. A
# host also DELIVERS the file to the viewer's browser — so a third party sees the
# viewer's IP address (the privacy notice says so, and gains a paragraph the moment
# any driver is set), and a playback url is a bearer once it is in a browser.
# Identity documents NEVER go to a host, on any setting.
#
# The hosts are NOT interchangeable, so there is one variable per KIND of media
# (VIDEO_STORAGE.md §10.5–10.6 has the table these lines summarise):
#
#   filemoon    video: a playback url per file, HLS or progressive. Its non-video
#               uploads are download-only, which is why it only ever gets video.
#   pixeldrain  any file: direct urls with byte ranges and a REAL delete — the one
#               host here that was not built around a single medium. Its free plan
#               refuses requests it reads as hotlinks (403 hotlink_detected), and a
#               store page loading its urls is that, so a paid plan is what the
#               delivery path needs. API keys also expire 30 days after last use.
#   telegraph   images only, 5 MB per file, permanent and free — and UNDOCUMENTED,
#               with NO DELETE. A store could never take an image back, so an image
#               sent there stays public forever. Probe it before trusting it:
#               npm run video:check --prefix app -- --probe-telegraph
#   catbox      DEVELOPMENT ONLY. Its operator's own terms forbid being a service's
#               CDN and say datacenter uploads may be filtered or purged.
#   antmedia    LIVE ONLY — it is not a storage host at all. It runs a seller's stream,
#               which nothing else here can do; no kind of file is routed to it, because
#               it declares no kind. Free software you install yourself (VIDEO_STORAGE.md
#               §12), so there is no per-minute meter and nobody else's account in the
#               middle — the cost is a machine and its bandwidth, which is the broadcast's
#               own size rather than a vendor's cut.
#
# VIDEO_DRIVER=filemoon            # filemoon | pixeldrain | catbox
# IMAGE_DRIVER=telegraph           # telegraph | pixeldrain
# FILE_DRIVER=pixeldrain           # pixeldrain (audio, archives, documents, video)
# LIVE_DRIVER=antmedia             # the only live host in the registry: free software on a
#                                  # machine you own. A metered hosted service was here and was
#                                  # removed (below) — this seam takes one value now.
#                                  # Unset = a seller pastes a playlist they got elsewhere.
#
# A kind with no driver declared stays on our disk — including every image, until
# IMAGE_DRIVER is set. That is the default and it is a safe one.
#
# LIVE_DRIVER is a different question from the three above: it does not decide where
# bytes live, it decides who runs the stream. The live file it produces is still a file
# with an .m3u8 in `external_url`, so the player, the breaks and the unlock ladder behave
# exactly as they do for a playlist a seller pasted themselves.
#
# ── Filemoon (the video host this product deploys with) ─────────────────────
# FILEMOON_API_BASE=https://filemoon.org/api/v1
# FILEMOON_TOKEN=                  # `id|secret`, the whole token, as issued
#
# ── api.video — REMOVED ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The metered live host: free sandbox, per-viewer-minute billing in production, and a
# payment method needed before a store could broadcast for real. Replaced by Ant Media
# Server on a machine of our own — free software, our bandwidth. Its key is no longer
# read by anything; the module and its stub were removed with it (VIDEO_STORAGE.md §11
# keeps the research, marked as history).
#
# ── MEDIA_RELAY — whose connection the bytes travel over ─────────────────────
# A redirect puts the viewer's browser on the host's connection and costs us nothing; a
# relay pipes every byte through this server, which is the only way to serve a host that
# refuses browser fetches. Pixeldrain's free plan answers 403 `hotlink_detected` to what it
# reads as a hotlink, and a store page redirecting to it is exactly that — so Pixeldrain is
# relayed by default (`capabilities.hotlink: 'refused-when-free'`).
#
#   unset                → relay the hosts that declare the refusal (today: Pixeldrain)
#   none                 → nobody: every remote file is a 302 (right for a Pro account)
#   all                  → every host that stores a file
#   pixeldrain,catbox    → exactly those
#
# Catbox is NOT relayed by default, and that is a licence decision rather than a technical
# one: their terms forbid being a service's CDN, and piping their bytes through us is still
# that. The relay fixes HTTP problems, not terms.
#
# MEDIA_RELAY=
#
# ── MEDIA_FALLBACK — where a file goes when the first host says no ───────────
# A host refusing is not hypothetical: Pixeldrain's keys expire 30 days after their last use
# and its free plan refuses what it reads as abuse; Catbox's terms are enforced by a person
# rather than by a status code; any host can have an afternoon. Without this, a refusal means
# the file lands on our own disk — which works, and wastes every other host you configured.
#
#   unset                → each kind tries only its own driver, then our own disk
#   all                  → every configured host that takes that kind, in registry order
#   pixeldrain,catbox    → exactly those, after the primary
#
# A live host never appears in the chain (it stores no files), a host that does not take the
# kind is never asked, and every refusal it met is logged with the host's own words — so a
# host that refuses everything is visible rather than quietly routed around.
#
# MEDIA_FALLBACK=
#
# ── MEDIA_EDGE_* — delivering a host's bytes without paying for them ─────────
# The relay in the application works, and it sends every byte of somebody else's file out through
# YOUR upload — from ONE address, which is the traffic shape a host's abuse detection is built to
# notice. Cloudflare's Workers have no egress charge and many addresses, so for the two hosts with
# known refusals (Pixeldrain's free plan 403s a browser fetch; Catbox's terms forbid the traffic
# shape entirely) a signed redirect to a Worker is the cheap delivery path.
#
# It is NOT an open relay, and that is why the secret is required rather than optional: every url
# carries an HMAC over `host/id/expiry` that the Worker verifies before it opens a socket. A base
# url without a secret is treated as not configured — the tempting half-setup (sign nothing, serve
# anyone) cannot happen by setting one variable. Read `ci/cloudflare/README.md` before deploying.
#
# MEDIA_EDGE_BASE=https://bytebikri-media.<your-subdomain>.workers.dev
# MEDIA_EDGE_SECRET=               # same value as `wrangler secret put MEDIA_EDGE_SECRET`
# MEDIA_EDGE_TTL_SECONDS=21600     # how long a signed link works; six hours by default
#
# ── Ant Media Server — the free live host (VIDEO_STORAGE.md §12) ─────────────
# The opposite trade from api.video: no per-minute bill and nobody's account in the middle,
# in exchange for a machine you run and the bandwidth it uses. Community Edition is
# Apache-licensed, free forever, and commercial use is allowed — this is the one to install.
#
#   sudo ./install_ant-media-server.sh
#   panel   https://<host>:5443          REST  https://<host>:5443/<app>/rest/v2
#   play    https://<host>:5443/<app>/streams/<streamId>.m3u8
#   publish rtmp://<host>:1935/<app>/<streamId>
#
# Do NOT put an Enterprise TRIAL key in production: the trial is evaluation-only by its own
# EULA (no commercial use, no benefiting a third party), and hosting other sellers' stores
# is commercial. Free educational/community licences exist if Enterprise features are ever
# needed; Community needs none.
#
# ANT_MEDIA_BASE=https://stream.example.com:5443
# ANT_MEDIA_APP=LiveApp            # the app name in the panel; default is LiveApp
# ANT_MEDIA_REST_SECRET=           # set only if jwtControlEnabled is on; we sign HS256 per call
# ANT_MEDIA_RTMP_BASE=rtmp://stream.example.com:1935/LiveApp
#
# A missing REST secret means the server authorises by IP filter, and that is normal for a
# fresh install: the calls then come from the address the panel trusts. Either way the
# streamId is minted here from 128 random bits, because on Community Edition it is the
# publish credential itself — whoever knows it can go live on that slot.
#
# ── Pixeldrain ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PIXELDRAIN_API_BASE=https://pixeldrain.com/api     # note the /api — it is part of it
# PIXELDRAIN_API_KEY=               # sent as HTTP Basic, in the PASSWORD field
#
# ── Telegra.ph ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TELEGRAPH_UPLOAD_BASE=https://telegra.ph/upload    # NOT api.telegra.ph — that host
# TELEGRAPH_FILE_BASE=https://telegra.ph             # refuses the undocumented upload node
#
# ── Catbox ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CATBOX_API_BASE=https://catbox.moe/user/api.php
# CATBOX_FILE_BASE=https://files.catbox.moe
# CATBOX_USERHASH=                 # uploads without it are anonymous AND undeletable
#
# ── Mastodon (DISTRIBUTION, not storage — LIVE_DISTRIBUTION.md) ─────────────
# An audience, not a host. A post federates to followers on OTHER instances, which is how
# a store gets found by people who never visited it; the live video itself is elsewhere
# (§11). Mastodon has NO live streaming and NO HLS — it cannot carry a stream, only news
# of one. Tokens do not expire: revocation or deletion is the only thing that ends one.
#
# APP REGISTRATION: registered with the out-of-band redirect
# (`urn:ietf:wg:oauth:2.0:oob`) this application can serve ONE account, whose owner
# pastes its own token. Letting each seller connect their own account needs an https
# redirect URI added in the Mastodon dashboard — which shape we want is a product
# decision, and the first item in LIVE_DISTRIBUTION.md §6.
#
# MASTODON_BASE=https://mastodon.social
# MASTODON_CLIENT_KEY=
# MASTODON_CLIENT_SECRET=
# MASTODON_ACCESS_TOKEN=            # server-side only: never client code, never a url,
#                                   # never logged, never in an audit payload
#
# Check whichever one is configured, from a machine that can reach it:
#   npm run video:check --prefix app -- --drivers          # the routing table + terms
#   npm run video:check --prefix app -- --driver=pixeldrain --upload a.mp3
#   npm run video:check --prefix app -- --probe-telegraph  # is the image node alive?
#   npm run video:check --prefix app -- --probe-live       # mint a live stream, print the
#                                                          # ingest addresses, remove it
#   npm run mastodon:check --prefix app                    # the account, and the instance's
#                                                          # real caps (prints no secrets)
#   npm run mastodon:check --prefix app -- --dry-run        # what an announcement would say
